Simulation Methodology

Q-HISS Scientific Publication Edition — v1.0

This section specifies a repeatable, audit-friendly simulation methodology to validate Q-HISS across navigation, autonomy, energy/thermal management, fault tolerance, and governance. It is designed to be implemented as an open research pipeline (Python + modular models) and to produce results acceptable for scientific review (clear assumptions, metrics, and statistical rigor).

⸻

1. Goals and Evaluation Questions

Primary research questions (RQs)

RQ1 — Autonomy under latency: Can Q-HISS maintain mission objectives under communication delays and limited ground intervention?
RQ2 — Resilience: How robust is the system to faults, radiation events, and sensor degradation?
RQ3 — Resource optimization: Does the energy/thermal scheduler keep the platform within safe bounds while maximizing task completion?
RQ4 — Governed autonomy: Does the governance layer prevent unauthorized actions with zero safety-rule violations?
RQ5 — Mission continuity: What is the probability of mission success under uncertainty (Monte Carlo)?

⸻

2. Simulation Approach Overview

We use a multi-fidelity, hybrid simulation approach:

2.1 Multi-physics + autonomy co-simulation
   •   Flight dynamics: translational + rotational dynamics (continuous-time)
   •   Navigation estimation: multi-rate EKF/UKF + health-state estimation
   •   Autonomy & planning: hierarchical controller (reflex + cognitive planner + governance gate)
   •   Resource networks: discrete-time energy + thermal network models
   •   Fault/Radiation: event-driven fault injection + dose models
   •   Comms: delay/loss models affecting command and telemetry

2.2 Fidelity tiers (to retire risk progressively)

Tier 0: functional mock models (fast iteration)
Tier 1: physics-based orbit/attitude + simple thermal/power
Tier 2: higher fidelity thermal network + realistic actuator/sensor noise
Tier 3: hardware-in-the-loop proxies (optional later)

