6. Fault Injection Methodology

Fault injection is performed as event-driven perturbations in time:

6.1 Fault taxonomy

F1: sensor faults (bias, dropout, degradation)
F2: actuator faults (loss of authority, saturation, stuck-on/off)
F3: compute faults (latency spikes, reset events, degraded performance)
F4: power faults (battery SOH decline, array degradation)
F5: thermal faults (radiator efficiency loss, heater failure)
F6: comm faults (high delay, packet loss, blackout windows)

6.2 Fault schedule generation

For Monte Carlo run i, sample a fault set:
\mathcal{F}^{(i)} = \{(t_k, type_k, severity_k, duration_k)\}_{k=1}^{K}
Severities follow controlled distributions (mild/moderate/severe), e.g.:
   •   sensor bias: \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_b^2)
   •   dropout rate: Bernoulli with p
   •   radiation event: Poisson arrivals with intensity distribution

6.3 Recovery validation

Measure detection delay and recovery success:
   •   time-to-detect
   •   time-to-recover
   •   residual risk after recovery
   •   mode transition correctness

