7. Governance Layer Validation

Governance is validated as a formal action gate:

7.1 Action classes
   •   \mathcal{U}_{free}: routine actions
   •   \mathcal{U}_{auth}: high-risk or irreversible actions requiring authorization token

7.2 Test cases
   •   Attempt unauthorized high-risk maneuvers → must be blocked
   •   Attempt risky mode changes under invalid policy state → must be blocked
   •   Audit trail completeness → must be replayable

7.3 Governance metrics
   •   Unauthorized action attempts blocked (%)
   •   Unauthorized action success count (target 0)
   •   Safety constraint violations (target 0)
   •   Audit log integrity (hash chain continuity)

⸻

8. Monte Carlo Campaign Design

8.1 Experimental design

Run a Monte Carlo suite:
   •   N = 500–10{,}000 trials depending on complexity
   •   Fixed scenario + randomized disturbances and faults
   •   Controlled seeds for reproducibility

8.2 Comparative baselines (ablation study)

To demonstrate impact of stacked architecture, compare variants:

B0: Classical control only (no planner)
B1: Planner without neuromorphic reflex
B2: Planner + reflex, without governance gate
B3: Full Q-HISS (reflex + planner + governance + audit)

This isolates where improvements come from.

